#🚀 Task : Basic Stream Selectors
🎯 Objective
Master label-based stream selection to filter logs efficiently before analyzing content.

📖 Understanding Stream Selectors
LogQL queries start with label selectors in curly braces:

{container_name="sample-app"}
Label Operators:

= - Exact match
!= - Not equal
=~ - Regex match
!~ - Regex not match
🔧 Step 1: Generate Sample Logs
First, generate diverse logs from the sample app:


for i in {1..5}; do
  curl -s http://localhost:5000/generate-logs
  sleep 2
done
This generates INFO, WARNING, and ERROR logs with various patterns.

🔍 Step 2: Query All Sample App Logs
In Grafana Explore, try this basic query:

{container_name="sample-app"}
Or via API:

curl -G -s 'http://localhost:3100/loki/api/v1/query_range' --data-urlencode 'query={container_name="sample-app"}' --data-urlencode 'limit=10' | jq '.data.result'
⚡ Step 3: Filter by Log Level
Try these queries in Grafana Explore:

Only ERROR logs:

{container_name="sample-app", extracted_level="ERROR"}
WARNING and ERROR logs (regex):

{container_name="sample-app", extracted_level=~"WARNING|ERROR"}
Exclude INFO logs:

{container_name="sample-app", extracted_level!="INFO"}
✅ Success Criteria: You should see different log volumes when filtering by level. ERROR logs should be much fewer than INFO logs.


Please ensure that you click on Grafana at the top of the terminal. When prompted for credentials, use the Username and Password as admin and admin, respectively.

#Task : Content Filtering & Text Search
🎯 Objective
Learn to search log content using text filters and regex patterns.

📖 Line Filter Operators
After selecting streams with labels, filter content with these operators:

|= - Contains text
!= - Does not contain
|~ - Regex match
!~ - Regex not match
💻 Practice Queries
Find database-related logs:

{container_name="sample-app"} |= "database"
Find payment or order logs:

{container_name="sample-app"} |~ "payment|order"
Exclude health check logs:

{container_name="sample-app"} != "health"
Chain filters (database errors only):

{container_name="sample-app"} |= "database" |~ "error|failed|timeout"
💡 Challenge: Try to find all logs containing "timeout" OR "failed" using regex. Then exclude any that contain "retry".

Please ensure that you click on Grafana at the top of the terminal. When prompted for credentials, use the Username and Password as admin and admin, respectively.

#📊 Task 4: Deriving Metrics from Logs
🎯 Objective
Transform logs into metrics using aggregation functions.

📖 Metric Functions
LogQL can derive metrics from log data:

count_over_time() - Count log lines
rate() - Calculate per-second rate
avg_over_time() - Average numeric values
sum_over_time() - Sum values
quantile_over_time() - Calculate percentiles
💻 Example Queries
Count errors in last 5 minutes:

count_over_time({extracted_level="ERROR"}[5m])
Error rate (errors per second):

sum(rate({extracted_level="ERROR"}[1m]))
Average response time:

avg_over_time({container_name="sample-app"} | json | unwrap response_time_ms [5m])
95th percentile response time:

quantile_over_time(0.95, {container_name="sample-app"} | json | unwrap response_time_ms [5m])
Error rate by container:

sum by(container_name) (rate({extracted_level="ERROR"}[1m]))
💡 Try It: Create a query to calculate the percentage of 5xx errors out of total requests in the last 5 minutes.
 sum(rate({container_name="sample-app"} |= " 5" [5m]))
