#🔍 Task 1: Understanding Correlation Workflows
🎯 Objective
Understand why correlation is critical and learn the workflow for investigating incidents using unified observability.

📖 Real-World Incident Scenario
The Problem:

At 3:15 PM, your monitoring dashboard shows a sudden spike in HTTP 500 errors for the payment service. The error rate jumped from 0% to 15%. Users are complaining that payments are failing.

Traditional Approach (30-45 minutes):

Notice the spike in the dashboard
Manually open the Grafana Explore view
Guess which logs to search (payment service?)
Try to remember the exact time of the spike
Manually adjust the time range
Try different LogQL queries to find errors
Eventually find the root cause after 30+ minutes
Unified Observability Approach (< 3 minutes):

Click on the metric spike
Automatically navigate to Explore with pre-filled LogQL query for payment service
Time range automatically set to spike moment
Immediately see ERROR logs: "Database connection pool exhausted"
Root cause identified in under 3 minutes!
💡 Key Insight: Correlation eliminates manual steps and guesswork, enabling you to go from "what's wrong" to "why it's wrong" in seconds.

#🏷️ Task 2: Establish Consistent Labeling
🎯 Objective
Review and verify that your metrics and logs share consistent labels for correlation.

📋 Step 1: Review Prometheus Labels
First, check what labels are available in your Prometheus metrics:

curl -s http://localhost:9090/api/v1/labels | jq '.data'
Common labels you should see: job, instance, container

📋 Step 2: Review Loki Labels
Check what labels are available in your Loki logs:

curl -s http://localhost:3100/loki/api/v1/labels | jq '.data'
Common labels you should see: container_name, source, extracted_level

✅ Step 3: Verify Label Overlap
Check if there are common labels between Prometheus and Loki. The container_name label should exist in both.

Query Prometheus for container metrics:

curl -s 'http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query?query=up' | jq '.data.result[].metric'
Query Loki for container names:

curl -s 'http://localhost:3100/loki/api/v1/label/container_name/values' | jq '.data'
🎯 Success Criteria: You should see overlapping container identifiers in both systems. This is what enables correlation!

#Task 3: Configure Data Links
🎯 Objective
Create data links that allow you to click on a metric in a dashboard and automatically navigate to the relevant logs in Grafana Explore.

📖 Understanding Data Links
Data links in Grafana allow you to create clickable links from dashboard panels. They can:

Use variables from the metric labels (like ${__field.labels.container})
Use time range variables (like ${__from} and ${__to})
Open Grafana Explore with a pre-filled LogQL query
Automatically set the correct time range
🛠️ Step 1: Access Grafana
Open Grafana in your browser (the URL will be provided in your lab environment). Log in with:

Username: admin
Password: admin
🎨 Step 2: Create or Open a Dashboard
Create a new dashboard with name `metric dashboard` that displays Prometheus metrics. Add a panel that shows a metric like:

rate(http_requests_total[5m])
Or any other metric from your sample application.

🔗 Step 3: Add Data Link
Edit the panel and navigate to the Panel options > Data links section. Add a new data link with:

Title: View Logs

URL:

/explore?left={"datasource":"Loki","queries":[{"expr":"{container_name=\"sample-app\"}"}],"range":{"from":"${__from}","to":"${__to}"}}
This creates a link that:

Opens Grafana Explore
Selects Loki as the data source
Pre-fills a LogQL query for the sample-app container
Sets the time range to match your dashboard
✅ Step 4: Test the Data Link
Save the panel and return to the dashboard view. Hover over a data point in your graph and you should see a "View Logs" link appear. Click it to verify that it opens Explore with the correct query and time range.

💡 Advanced: You can use ${__field.labels.container_name} to dynamically use the container label from the clicked metric, making the link work for multiple services automatically!

#Task 4: Build Split-View Dashboards
🎯 Objective
Create a unified dashboard that displays metrics and logs side-by-side with synchronized time ranges.

🎨 Step 1: Create New Dashboard
In Grafana, create a new dashboard titled "Unified Observability - Metrics & Logs."

📈 Step 2: Add Metrics Panel
Add a panel at the top to display key metrics. Example queries include:

HTTP Request Rate: rate(http_requests_total[5m])
HTTP Error Rate: rate(http_errors_total[5m])
Total Requests: sum(http_requests_total) by (status)
Total Errors: sum(http_errors_total)
📝 Step 3: Add Logs Panel
Below the metrics panel, add a new panel:

Select Loki as the data source.
Use visualization type: Logs.
Enter a LogQL query: {container_name="sample-app"} | json.
This panel will show logs from the same service as your metrics.
⏱️ Step 4: Configure Time Sync
Both panels will automatically utilize the dashboard's time range picker. When you adjust the time range at the top of the dashboard, both metrics and logs will update to reflect data for that specific period.

#🚨 Task 5: Real-World Incident Investigation
🎯 Objective
Apply your correlation skills to investigate a simulated production incident from metric spike to root cause.

🚨 Incident Scenario
Alert Received:

"HIGH ERROR RATE: sample-app service showing 15% error rate (threshold: 5%)"

Your Mission:

Use your unified observability dashboard to identify the root cause in under 3 minutes.

🔍 Investigation Steps
Step 1: Generate Sample Incident

curl http://localhost:5000/simulate-incident
This endpoint generates a burst of errors to simulate a real incident.

Step 2: Identify the Spike

Open your unified dashboard
Observe the error rate metric spike
Note the exact time of the spike
Step 3: Correlate to Logs

Click on the spike or use your data link
Navigate to the logs for that exact time period
Filter for ERROR level logs
Step 4: Analyze Error Messages

Use LogQL to parse and filter: {container_name="sample-app", extracted_level="ERROR"} | json
Look for common error patterns
Identify the root cause message
Step 5: Verify Context

Click on an error log line
Use "Show Context" to see surrounding logs
Understand what happened before and after the error
💡 Common Root Causes to Look for:

"Database connection pool exhausted"
"Timeout connecting to external API"
"Out of memory error"
"Failed to acquire lock"
"Disk full"
✅ Mission Complete! If you identified the root cause in under 3 minutes, you've successfully demonstrated 10x faster incident diagnosis through unified observability!

#Intelligent Alerting Alertmanager

Task 2: Create Prometheus Alert Rules
🎯 Objective
Create the alert_rules.yml file with exactly 4 alert rules that monitor error rate, latency, service uptime, and memory usage.

📝 Instructions
In the file /root/assets/prometheus/alert_rules.yml, create a new alert group named application_alerts with an interval of 30s. Add the following 4 alerts with the exact expressions, thresholds, durations, labels, and annotations specified below:

1. HighErrorRate

Expression: rate(http_errors_total[5m]) > 0.05
Duration: 1m
Labels: severity=critical, team=backend, service=sample-app
Annotations:
summary: “High error rate detected on {{ $labels.instance }}”
description: “Error rate is {{ $value | humanize }} errors/sec on {{ $labels.instance }}. Check application logs immediately.”
runbook_url: “https://runbooks.example.com/high-error-rate”
2. HighRequestLatency

Expression: histogram_quantile(0.95,  rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])) > 0.5
Duration: 2m
Labels: severity=warning, team=backend, service=sample-app
Annotations:
summary: “High request latency on {{ $labels.instance }}”
description: “95th percentile latency is {{ $value | humanize }}s on {{ $labels.instance }}. Performance degradation detected.”
runbook_url: “https://runbooks.example.com/high-latency”
3. ServiceDown

Expression: up{job="sample-app"} == 0
Duration: 30s
Labels: severity=critical, team=platform, service=sample-app
Annotations:
summary: “Service {{ $labels.job }} is down”
description: “Instance {{ $labels.instance }} has been down for more than 30 seconds. Immediate action required.”
runbook_url: “https://runbooks.example.com/service-down”
4. HighMemoryUsage

Expression: (node_memory_MemTotal_bytes - node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes) / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes * 100 > 80
Duration: 2m
Labels: severity=warning, team=platform, service=infrastructure
Annotations:
summary: “High memory usage on {{ $labels.instance }}”
description: “Memory usage is {{ $value | humanize }}% on {{ $labels.instance }}. Consider scaling or investigating memory leaks.”
runbook_url: “https://runbooks.example.com/high-memory”
Ensure your YAML structure is valid, the indentation is correct, and all values are utilized exactly as specified.


Note: Please review the description carefully to gain a thorough understanding of the alert_rules.yaml file. Based on your insights, proceed to construct a new file.

Ans:
Perfect — below is the **exact correctly structured YAML file** with:

* ✅ One group: `application_alerts`
* ✅ `interval: 30s`
* ✅ Exactly 4 alerts
* ✅ Exact expressions
* ✅ Exact durations
* ✅ Exact labels
* ✅ Exact annotations (including templating and URLs)
* ✅ Proper indentation
* ✅ Valid Prometheus syntax

---

# 📄 `/root/assets/prometheus/alert_rules.yml`

```yaml
groups:
  - name: application_alerts
    interval: 30s
    rules:

      - alert: HighErrorRate
        expr: rate(http_errors_total[5m]) > 0.05
        for: 1m
        labels:
          severity: critical
          team: backend
          service: sample-app
        annotations:
          summary: "High error rate detected on {{ $labels.instance }}"
          description: "Error rate is {{ $value | humanize }} errors/sec on {{ $labels.instance }}. Check application logs immediately."
          runbook_url: "https://runbooks.example.com/high-error-rate"

      - alert: HighRequestLatency
        expr: histogram_quantile(0.95,  rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])) > 0.5
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          team: backend
          service: sample-app
        annotations:
          summary: "High request latency on {{ $labels.instance }}"
          description: "95th percentile latency is {{ $value | humanize }}s on {{ $labels.instance }}. Performance degradation detected."
          runbook_url: "https://runbooks.example.com/high-latency"

      - alert: ServiceDown
        expr: up{job="sample-app"} == 0
        for: 30s
        labels:
          severity: critical
          team: platform
          service: sample-app
        annotations:
          summary: "Service {{ $labels.job }} is down"
          description: "Instance {{ $labels.instance }} has been down for more than 30 seconds. Immediate action required."
          runbook_url: "https://runbooks.example.com/service-down"

      - alert: HighMemoryUsage
        expr: (node_memory_MemTotal_bytes - node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes) / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes * 100 > 80
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: warning
          team: platform
          service: infrastructure
        annotations:
          summary: "High memory usage on {{ $labels.instance }}"
          description: "Memory usage is {{ $value | humanize }}% on {{ $labels.instance }}. Consider scaling or investigating memory leaks."
          runbook_url: "https://runbooks.example.com/high-memory"
```

---

# ✅ After Creating the File

Run:

```bash
promtool check rules /root/assets/prometheus/alert_rules.yml
```Not installaed promtool
